  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA taste of fall expected today as Canadian high pressure quickly \npushes eastward across the region. Dry conditions persist through \nthe day today. High temperatures will struggle to reach 70 degrees \nacross much of the forecast area with low 70s most likely across the \nI-75 corridor. Chilly low temperatures tonight in the upper 40s to \nlower 50s. As the high exits to the east, southerly return flow will \nput the region back into the warm sector by Tuesday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nLow pressure moving east across Ontario will lift a warm front \nacross the forecast area on Tuesday. The bulk of the area should \nremain dry during the day on Tuesday with the exception of zones in \nNorthwest Ohio. Scattered strong to severe storms will approach from \nthe west Tuesday afternoon and evening bringing a localized risk of \ndamaging wind gusts. Counties along the I-75 corridor are in a \nMarginal Risk (Level 1 of 5) for severe storms on Tuesday. Locally \nheavy rainfall is possible across Northwest Ohio given PWATs \nincreasing to 1.75-2.00 inches Tuesday evening. \n\nThe system will then drag a cold front east across the region \nTuesday night before the front stalls across the region Wednesday \nand Thursday. Scattered showers and thunderstorms are expected both \ndays as the stationary front remains draped across the Northern Ohio \nValley. Showers and storms will be capable of producing heavy \nrainfall across the region as areawide PWATs range between 1.75-2.25 \ninches through mid/late week. The WPC has our entire forecast area \nunder a Marginal Risk (Level 1 of 4) for excessive rainfall \nWednesday and Thursday. The stationary front will dive south \nThursday night into Friday allowing for any remaining precipitation \nchances to decrease by the end of the week. \n\nHighs temperatures through this week will rise into the upper 70s to \nlower 80s with warm overnight lows in the mid 60s.",
